WEBVTT

00:00.530 --> 00:02.240
- [Instructor] You've
likely heard the concept

00:02.240 --> 00:05.720
of even and odd numbers,
and what we're going to do

00:05.720 --> 00:09.720
in this video is think about
even and odd functions.

00:09.720 --> 00:12.290
And as you can see or as you will see,

00:12.290 --> 00:14.500
there's a little bit of a
parallel between the two,

00:14.500 --> 00:16.860
but there's also some differences.

00:16.860 --> 00:20.640
So let's first think about
what an even function is.

00:20.640 --> 00:23.500
One way to think about
an even function is that

00:23.500 --> 00:26.740
if you were to flip it over the y-axis,

00:26.740 --> 00:28.990
that the function looks the same.

00:28.990 --> 00:33.030
So here's a classic example
of an even function.

00:33.030 --> 00:35.870
It would be this right over here,

00:35.870 --> 00:38.270
your classic parabola

00:38.270 --> 00:40.510
where your vertex

00:40.510 --> 00:42.650
is on the y-axis.

00:42.650 --> 00:44.730
This is an even function.

00:44.730 --> 00:46.150
So this one is maybe the graph

00:46.150 --> 00:48.930
of f of x is equal to x squared.

00:48.930 --> 00:53.280
And notice, if you were to
flip it over the y-axis,

00:53.280 --> 00:56.220
you're going to get the exact same graph.

00:56.220 --> 00:58.980
Now, a way that we can talk
about that mathematically,

00:58.980 --> 01:00.200
and we've talked about this

01:00.200 --> 01:02.640
when we introduced the idea of reflection,

01:02.640 --> 01:05.040
to say that a function is equal

01:05.040 --> 01:08.440
to its reflection over the y-axis,

01:08.440 --> 01:13.300
that's just saying that f of
x is equal to f of negative x.

01:13.300 --> 01:15.820
Because if you were to replace
your x's with a negative x,

01:15.820 --> 01:20.190
that flips your function over the y-axis.

01:20.190 --> 01:22.930
Now, what about odd functions?

01:22.930 --> 01:25.310
So odd functions, you
get the same function

01:25.310 --> 01:29.040
if you flip over the y- and the x-axes.

01:29.040 --> 01:33.930
So let me draw a classic
example of an odd function.

01:33.930 --> 01:36.560
Our classic example would be

01:36.560 --> 01:40.420
f of x is equal to x to the third,

01:40.420 --> 01:42.000
is equal to x to the third,

01:42.000 --> 01:43.773
and it looks something like this.

01:44.750 --> 01:49.260
So notice, if you were to
flip first over the y-axis,

01:49.260 --> 01:51.810
you would get something
that looks like this.

01:51.810 --> 01:53.360
So I'll do it as a dotted line.

01:53.360 --> 01:55.580
If you were to flip just over the y-axis,

01:55.580 --> 01:56.467
it would look like this.

01:56.467 --> 01:59.780
And then if you were to
flip that over the x-axis,

01:59.780 --> 02:04.330
well, then you're going to
get the same function again.

02:04.330 --> 02:07.830
Now, how would we write
this down mathematically?

02:07.830 --> 02:11.920
Well, that means that our
function is equivalent to

02:12.840 --> 02:15.260
not only flipping it over the y-axis,

02:15.260 --> 02:16.680
which would be f of negative x,

02:16.680 --> 02:19.120
but then flipping that over the x-axis,

02:19.120 --> 02:21.550
which is just taking the negative of that.

02:21.550 --> 02:24.210
So this is doing two flips.

02:24.210 --> 02:26.270
So some of you might be noticing a pattern

02:26.270 --> 02:28.530
or think you might be on the
verge of seeing a pattern

02:28.530 --> 02:32.000
that connects the words even
and odd with the notions

02:32.000 --> 02:34.970
that we know from earlier
in our mathematical lives.

02:34.970 --> 02:37.200
I've just shown you an even function

02:37.200 --> 02:39.470
where the exponent is an even number,

02:39.470 --> 02:41.200
and I've just showed you an odd function

02:41.200 --> 02:43.680
where the exponent is an odd number.

02:43.680 --> 02:46.920
Now, I encourage you to try
out many, many more polynomials

02:46.920 --> 02:48.290
and try out the exponents,

02:48.290 --> 02:53.290
but it turns out that if you
just have f of x is equal to,

02:53.320 --> 02:56.870
if you just have f of x
is equal to x to the n,

02:56.870 --> 03:00.410
then this is going to be an
even function if n is even,

03:00.410 --> 03:03.290
and it's going to an odd
function if n is odd.

03:03.290 --> 03:04.940
So that's one connection.

03:04.940 --> 03:06.317
Now, some of you are thinking,

03:06.317 --> 03:07.857
"Wait, but there seem
to be a lot of functions

03:07.857 --> 03:10.170
"that are neither even nor odd."

03:10.170 --> 03:12.520
And that is indeed the case.

03:12.520 --> 03:16.853
For example, if you just had
the graph x squared plus two,

03:17.710 --> 03:21.590
this right over here is
still going to be even.

03:21.590 --> 03:22.680
'Cause if you flip it over,

03:22.680 --> 03:25.020
you have the symmetry around the y-axis.

03:25.020 --> 03:26.950
You're going to get back to itself.

03:26.950 --> 03:30.200
But if you had x minus two squared,

03:30.200 --> 03:33.000
which looks like this,

03:33.000 --> 03:35.930
x minus two, that would
shift two to the right,

03:35.930 --> 03:37.260
it'll look like that.

03:37.260 --> 03:40.180
That is no longer even.

03:40.180 --> 03:43.810
Because notice, if you
flip it over the y-axis,

03:43.810 --> 03:47.340
you're no longer getting
the same function.

03:47.340 --> 03:49.720
So it's not just the exponent.

03:49.720 --> 03:52.340
It also matters on the structure
of the expression itself.

03:52.340 --> 03:55.690
If you have something very
simple, like just x to the n,

03:55.690 --> 03:58.720
well, then that could be or
that would be even or odd

03:58.720 --> 04:00.850
depending on what your n is.

04:00.850 --> 04:04.360
Similarly, if we were
to shift this f of x,

04:04.360 --> 04:07.550
if we were to even shift
it up, it's no longer,

04:07.550 --> 04:09.810
it is no longer, so if
this is x to the third,

04:09.810 --> 04:11.580
let's say, plus three,

04:11.580 --> 04:14.040
this is no longer odd.

04:14.040 --> 04:18.190
Because you flip it over once,
you get right over there.

04:18.190 --> 04:22.230
But then you flip it again,
you're going to get this.

04:22.230 --> 04:25.330
You're going to get something like this.

04:25.330 --> 04:28.730
So you're no longer back
to your original function.

04:28.730 --> 04:30.510
Now, an interesting thing to think about,

04:30.510 --> 04:35.510
can you imagine a function
that is both even and odd?

04:36.130 --> 04:38.100
So I encourage you to pause that video,

04:38.100 --> 04:40.600
or pause the video and
try to think about it.

04:40.600 --> 04:44.190
Is there a function where f of
x is equal to f of negative x

04:44.190 --> 04:47.973
and f of x is equal to the
negative of f of negative x?

04:48.940 --> 04:50.730
Well, I'll give you a hint,

04:50.730 --> 04:52.590
or actually I'll just give you the answer.

04:52.590 --> 04:57.350
Imagine if f of x is just
equal to the constant zero.

04:57.350 --> 05:01.750
Notice, this thing is
just a horizontal line,

05:01.750 --> 05:04.830
just like that, at y is equal to zero.

05:04.830 --> 05:07.660
And if you flip it over the y-axis,

05:07.660 --> 05:09.210
you get back to where it was before.

05:09.210 --> 05:12.830
Then if you flip it over the x-axis,

05:12.830 --> 05:15.730
again, then you're still back
to where you were before.

05:15.730 --> 05:18.760
So this over here is both even and odd,

05:18.760 --> 05:20.953
a very interesting case.
